WEBVTT

00:04.990 --> 00:10.170
One of the project documents that we talked about in the last lecture was a business case.

00:10.420 --> 00:14.410
Well it's really important to have a good understanding of the business case because that can affect

00:14.410 --> 00:18.490
your entire project can affect where the project even is launched.

00:18.520 --> 00:23.420
So let's look at the characteristics of a business case and how it affects a project.

00:23.470 --> 00:28.240
First off of project business case is really an economic feasibility study.

00:28.270 --> 00:31.630
It's can we afford to do the project.

00:31.630 --> 00:33.840
Is it financially feasible.

00:33.910 --> 00:36.960
Does it make sense to invest in this project.

00:37.120 --> 00:41.740
So what's the validity of the project is it really going to create something that we can use and that's

00:41.740 --> 00:44.040
going to bring US business value.

00:44.140 --> 00:51.100
It helps to make future project management decisions and actions based on the finances in the business

00:51.100 --> 00:56.790
value that the project is going to create or that's a risk score that may not be of value.

00:56.800 --> 00:58.610
We're going to stay away from that.

00:58.660 --> 01:01.840
The business case is maintained throughout the project.

01:01.840 --> 01:06.800
So as there are new discoveries there are new additions there are some changes.

01:06.850 --> 01:13.700
Then you have to update the business case to show whether it's justified in need it or characteristics

01:13.700 --> 01:19.970
about the business case the project sponsor is accountable for the development in the maintenance of

01:19.970 --> 01:26.190
the business case not the project manager the project sponsor maintains the business case.

01:26.210 --> 01:32.690
Now the PM is responsible for making recommendations and by saying this is why we need this change or

01:32.690 --> 01:37.930
this supports our business value supports the vision or strategy or what.

01:38.090 --> 01:40.490
What's already been laid out in the business case.

01:40.610 --> 01:48.070
But it's the sponsor that maintains it and the business case could also be at a program level a program.

01:48.200 --> 01:55.340
You have lots of individual projects but the program oversees those individual projects.

01:56.120 --> 02:01.960
Let's talk about the business case and business needs before a project is launched.

02:02.000 --> 02:09.780
We can go into the business case or create a business case as to why do we need the project.

02:09.860 --> 02:12.550
So what's prompting the need for action.

02:12.740 --> 02:19.910
A business case defines the business problem or the opportunity that the price of the project is going

02:19.910 --> 02:22.130
to solve or get.

02:22.130 --> 02:23.890
So what's the problem.

02:23.900 --> 02:25.120
How are we solving it.

02:25.340 --> 02:30.410
Or is there an opportunity that's going to have some definite business value that we should go out and

02:30.410 --> 02:36.350
grab it and get it like a market window or there's some opportunity or the customer request us to do

02:36.350 --> 02:37.290
the project.

02:37.500 --> 02:39.890
And so that's good business value.

02:40.040 --> 02:45.800
It identifies the stakeholders that are affected their business stakeholders or people or groups that

02:45.800 --> 02:49.760
are affected by the project and they can also affect the project.

02:49.760 --> 02:54.490
So we try to identify the stakeholders as early as possible.

02:54.680 --> 03:02.240
The business case often will identify the scope of the project so it doesn't have to create a total

03:02.240 --> 03:03.430
project scope statement.

03:03.440 --> 03:07.940
But generally it's a high level scope of what are we talking about here.

03:08.000 --> 03:15.320
So if we were to do a business case for new software that we were going to create or the business case

03:15.320 --> 03:21.920
to replace all of our laptops in our organization or a business case to create and build a warehouse

03:22.310 --> 03:26.820
it would be pretty able to describe the scope of what we're doing.

03:26.870 --> 03:31.910
We're building software replacing laptops or we're building a warehouse.

03:32.000 --> 03:40.010
So the business case would describe that project in some level of detail a business case is also used

03:40.010 --> 03:43.790
in Project determination should we do the project or not.

03:43.790 --> 03:48.380
So a business case will look at the scope of the project the value it brings.

03:48.410 --> 03:53.610
Then how does it fit with the organization strategies its goals and objectives.

03:53.720 --> 04:00.080
If it doesn't support the strategies goals and objectives we probably don't need to be doing the project

04:01.590 --> 04:04.690
what about root cause for solving a problem.

04:04.710 --> 04:09.380
What are the root cause root cause is just that what's the problem and what's causing it.

04:09.540 --> 04:13.550
And then we can have a project to go and attack that root cause.

04:13.620 --> 04:15.350
What about an opportunity.

04:15.360 --> 04:19.280
So I talked about there's an opportunity that we're going to have business value.

04:19.410 --> 04:25.170
So where's the opportunity the market or a customer request is an opportunity.

04:25.170 --> 04:27.000
What about a gap analysis.

04:27.000 --> 04:34.140
So we want to do some construction with a new type of material but we don't really have the skills to

04:34.290 --> 04:35.880
do that type of material.

04:35.910 --> 04:41.780
So we have a gap analysis as part of our our project determination part of our business case.

04:41.820 --> 04:43.440
What are the known risks.

04:43.470 --> 04:50.160
So if you're doing a project in health care there all sorts of known risk in your application area or

04:50.160 --> 04:56.970
in I.T. or in construction that just by being in that discipline you have some insight into what risks

04:56.970 --> 04:58.100
are there.

04:58.620 --> 05:00.810
What are the critical success factors.

05:00.810 --> 05:02.460
How do you know you're successful.

05:02.610 --> 05:06.170
What would constitute success in this project.

05:06.170 --> 05:13.050
So really important to have that up front to have a vision into if these things are true that I know

05:13.050 --> 05:14.680
my project is successful.

05:14.760 --> 05:20.920
So the business case helps paint that picture gives us goals to work towards as the project manager.

05:21.000 --> 05:27.310
The last one here is some decision criteria that the business case can make a recommendation on.

05:27.420 --> 05:31.020
We should do this or should not do this as an organization.

05:31.200 --> 05:39.270
So some decision criteria a business case can also do an analysis of a situation.

05:39.270 --> 05:45.720
So what I'm talking about here is should we do it or should we not based on this opportunity based on

05:45.720 --> 05:49.830
this problem based on you know a customer request.

05:49.920 --> 05:51.840
So should we do it or should we not.

05:52.050 --> 05:57.980
So we're going to look at three elements recommendations that we make.

05:58.020 --> 06:06.160
We have required desired an optional required would be you must do it.

06:06.240 --> 06:07.220
You must do it.

06:07.230 --> 06:13.110
So I'm talking about here is we're creating a piece of software and the requirement is it has to be

06:13.110 --> 06:19.320
secure the data has to be secure so you must do it in order for the project to be successful.

06:19.320 --> 06:23.340
That's one of the criteria is that the data must be secure.

06:23.730 --> 06:25.020
Desired is.

06:25.110 --> 06:29.120
Well we would like this to come true but if it's not it's OK.

06:29.130 --> 06:35.400
So an example here of desired would be we would like for you to also make a mobile app that fits with

06:35.460 --> 06:37.490
the desktop app.

06:37.620 --> 06:42.060
But if you can't get to it now we can do it later or some flavor of that.

06:42.060 --> 06:44.020
So we want it to be desired.

06:44.040 --> 06:48.810
So it's it's a good idea but it's not or it's not a deal breaker for us.

06:48.840 --> 06:52.820
And the third one is just optionals not necessarily it's not essential.

06:52.980 --> 06:56.100
And that may be the ability to print from the mobile app.

06:56.220 --> 06:58.640
So not really something we're worried about.

06:59.010 --> 07:07.560
So required desired and optional then we get into the recommendation for a project.

07:07.560 --> 07:08.730
This is me in the future.

07:08.730 --> 07:09.800
I have a good feeling.

07:09.870 --> 07:11.790
I don't know a whole lot.

07:11.800 --> 07:15.380
Anyway let's talk about a recommendation for a project.

07:15.450 --> 07:23.470
So out of the business case we could say don't do the project do nothing just keep business as usual.

07:23.610 --> 07:25.790
Do the minimum work possible.

07:25.800 --> 07:27.210
Don't invest too much.

07:27.210 --> 07:35.040
Just do what the minimum to seize the opportunity the bare minimum or do the most you could do do more

07:35.310 --> 07:37.080
than the minimum work possible.

07:37.080 --> 07:40.940
So seize the opportunity and really go after it with gusto.

07:41.010 --> 07:46.140
So these are three typical recommendations often from a business analysts and they all sound kind of

07:46.140 --> 07:52.800
negative don't they do nothing to the minimum or just do more than the minimum work possible in your

07:52.800 --> 07:54.300
project.

07:54.300 --> 07:57.460
All right so that's the characteristics of a business case.

07:57.600 --> 07:58.090
Good job.
